{
 "id": "2016-fornara-measuring",
 "name": "Measuring Landscape Identity: European and Mediterranean IAT",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: two Landscape Identity Implicit Association Tests (LI-IAT) pair the words Self (I, Me, Mine, Myself) and Other (It, Other, Their, Them) with the paper's four European vs four Not-European landscape photos and four Mediterranean vs four Not-Mediterranean photos (Figures 1–4, CC BY 4.0), in the classic seven-block layout (20, 20, 20+40, 20, 20+40 trials), sorted with the E and I keys or two on-screen buttons. Then the two eight-figure self-categorisation items (overlap of me with Europe and with the Mediterranean, A–H) and a 1–5 pleasantness rating of all 16 landscapes. The page stores every trial and computes each D score (Greenwald, Nosek & Banaji 2003, built-in error penalty). Analysis as in the paper: one-sample t-tests of D against 0, correlation of D with the matching identity item, regression with gender and pleasantness differences as controls. Original study: 174 residents of Rome and Cagliari; both D scores positive (0.74 and 0.75).\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) runs online in a browser with keyboard or touch buttons instead of on a 13-inch lab laptop; (2) the order of the two IATs and, separately for each IAT, which combined pairing comes first are randomised per participant (the paper counterbalanced the combined-block order and does not state the IAT order); (3) the red X error feedback, 250 ms gap between trials and instruction wording are mine (the paper does not print them); (4) Figure 5 prints the Europe wording for both items, so the Mediterranean item uses the same sentence with \"the Mediterranean\"; the circle figures are redrawn; (5) trials over 10 s are dropped and the share of responses under 300 ms is flagged for exclusion, following Greenwald et al. (2003); (6) a residence question is added, and the education categories follow the sample description plus \"Other\".",
 "author": "Fornara, Dentale, Troffa & Piras",
 "year": "2016",
